rule failure_groups:
    """Which rotor classes the full-pool head cannot do, and how it reads against presto.

    The question, the three controls and why the MMFF94 statistics are medians
    are in `failure_groups.py`'s docstring. What belongs here: a median RMSE over
    a held-out pool is one number over a mixture, and the only useful form of it
    is an applicability domain. If the electronic classes the phase census named,
    anomeric, conjugated, biaryl, amide, gauche effect, carry the error, that is
    a statement about chemistry; if permuted class labels of the same sizes
    produce differences just as large, there is no domain to state.

    THE PERMUTED CONTROL IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE. Several classes hold fewer than
    a hundred molecules, and a class of that size drawn from one pool moves the
    median by an amount this cohort produces from nothing. Without the envelope,
    the smallest classes are exactly the ones that would look most affected.

    COST. Minutes, CPU, on files already on disk. No GPU.
    """
    input:
        script="failure_groups.py",
        encoder="espaloma_encoder.py",
        census="phase_census.py",
        per_molecule="data/espaloma_full/frozen/per_molecule.csv",
        profiles="data/espaloma_full/frozen/profiles.csv",
        cache="data/pool_scans.npz",
    output:
        table="data/failure_groups.csv",
        quarantine="data/failure_groups_quarantine.csv",
        classes="results/failure_classes.png",
        pca="results/failure_pca.png",
        gallery="results/failure_gallery.png",
    log:
        "logs/failure_groups.log",
    shell:
        """
        {ESPALOMA} {input.script} \\
          --per-molecule {input.per_molecule} \\
          --profiles {input.profiles} \\
          --cache {input.cache} \\
          --table {output.table} \\
          --quarantine {output.quarantine} \\
          --class-figure {output.classes} \\
          --pca-figure {output.pca} \\
          --gallery-figure {output.gallery} > {log} 2>&1
        """


rule training_curves:
    """What the two full-pool arms actually did while training.

    The per-molecule table reports where training ENDED. It does not say whether
    the run converged, whether the kept epoch sat on a plateau or a spike, or
    how far the validation curve had drifted from the training loss by the time
    early stopping fired. Those are the questions a reader of a learning curve
    asks, and the answer changes how much weight the headline carries.

    WHY BOTH ARMS ON ONE AXIS. Frozen kept epoch 188 at 2.72 h and fine-tuned
    kept epoch 288 at 4.36 h, and fine-tuning ended WORSE on the full pool while
    costing 60 percent more wall clock. A curve says whether that is overfitting,
    visible as the validation curve rising while the training loss falls, or a
    run that simply had not finished.

    COST. Seconds, CPU. No GPU.
    """
    input:
        script="plot_training_curves.py",
        frozen="data/espaloma_full/frozen/loss.csv",
        finetuned="data/espaloma_full/finetuned_reference/loss.csv",
        frozen_molecules="data/espaloma_full/frozen/per_molecule.csv",
        finetuned_molecules="data/espaloma_full/finetuned_reference/per_molecule.csv",
    output:
        figure="results/training_curves.png",
        table="data/training_curves.csv",
    log:
        "logs/training_curves.log",
    shell:
        """
        {ESPALOMA} {input.script} \\
          --loss frozen={input.frozen} finetuned={input.finetuned} \\
          --per-molecule frozen={input.frozen_molecules} \\
             finetuned={input.finetuned_molecules} \\
          --figure {output.figure} \\
          --table {output.table} > {log} 2>&1
        """


rule full_pool_arms:
    """Frozen against fine-tuned on the full pool, paired on the same molecules.

    The question, and why the change is measured per molecule rather than
    between two medians, are in `compare_arms.py`'s docstring. What belongs
    here: the two arms shared one split and one seed, so every held-out molecule
    has a frozen and a fine-tuned prediction and the comparison is PAIRED.
    Quoting the two medians and their separate intervals instead would measure
    the width of the pool rather than the difference between the arms, which is
    the mistake this project has already made once on a seed sweep.

    THE ARMS RAN AS SEPARATE ARRAY TASKS, so each wrote its own table with its
    own `encoder` column. They are concatenated here rather than in either
    script, because neither script should have to know how the campaign was
    scheduled.

    COST. Seconds, CPU. No GPU.
    """
    input:
        script="compare_arms.py",
        frozen_molecules="data/espaloma_full/frozen/per_molecule.csv",
        finetuned_molecules="data/espaloma_full/finetuned_reference/per_molecule.csv",
        frozen_profiles="data/espaloma_full/frozen/profiles.csv",
        finetuned_profiles="data/espaloma_full/finetuned_reference/profiles.csv",
        frozen_loss="data/espaloma_full/frozen/loss.csv",
        finetuned_loss="data/espaloma_full/finetuned_reference/loss.csv",
    output:
        molecules="data/espaloma_full/both_per_molecule.csv",
        profiles="data/espaloma_full/both_profiles.csv",
        loss="data/espaloma_full/both_loss.csv",
        curves="results/full_pool_curves.png",
        before_after="results/full_pool_before_after.png",
        profile_figure="results/full_pool_profiles.png",
        summary="data/full_pool_arms.csv",
    log:
        "logs/full_pool_arms.log",
    shell:
        """
        for pair in "{input.frozen_molecules} {input.finetuned_molecules} {output.molecules}" \
                    "{input.frozen_profiles} {input.finetuned_profiles} {output.profiles}" \
                    "{input.frozen_loss} {input.finetuned_loss} {output.loss}"; do
          set -- $pair
          head -1 "$1" > "$3"
          tail -n +2 "$1" >> "$3"
          tail -n +2 "$2" >> "$3"
        done
        {ESPALOMA} {input.script} \\
          --history {output.loss} \\
          --per-molecule {output.molecules} \\
          --profiles {output.profiles} \\
          --curves {output.curves} \\
          --before-after {output.before_after} \\
          --profile-figure {output.profile_figure} \\
          --summary {output.summary} > {log} 2>&1
        """


rule chemical_clusters:
    """Does any cluster of this cohort carry the error, found rather than named.

    The question, why Butina rather than k-means and why the clustering is done
    in fingerprint space rather than in a projection are in
    `chemical_clusters.py`'s docstring. What belongs here: the class table in
    `failure_groups` can only find chemistry somebody thought to test for. This
    tests every cluster of thirty or more, so a failure region nobody named gets
    named, and a class table that already found everything is confirmed by this
    finding nothing beyond it.

    THE THREE PROJECTIONS DECIDE NOTHING. PCA, t-SNE and UMAP are drawn from one
    set of fingerprints and coloured by one set of Butina labels, so they can be
    compared; every number comes from the 2048-bit space. A cluster tight in one
    projection and scattered in the other two is that projection's doing, which
    is the reason for drawing all three rather than picking the flattering one.

    COST. Minutes, CPU. The pairwise Tanimoto matrix over 3,698 molecules is
    about 6.8 million distances and t-SNE is the slow part. No GPU.
    """
    input:
        script="chemical_clusters.py",
        encoder="espaloma_encoder.py",
        census="phase_census.py",
        per_molecule="data/espaloma_full/frozen/per_molecule.csv",
        profiles="data/espaloma_full/frozen/profiles.csv",
        cache="data/pool_scans.npz",
    output:
        table="data/chemical_clusters.csv",
        figure="results/cluster_projections.png",
        clusters="results/cluster_forest.png",
    log:
        "logs/chemical_clusters.log",
    shell:
        """
        {UMAP} {input.script} \\
          --per-molecule {input.per_molecule} \\
          --profiles {input.profiles} \\
          --cache {input.cache} \\
          --table {output.table} \\
          --figure {output.figure} \\
          --cluster-figure {output.clusters} > {log} 2>&1
        """


rule presto_comparison:
    """Our head on TorsionNet500, in the column presto and espaloma report in.

    The question, what it does and does not settle, and why the reference lines
    are drawn as lines rather than as competing points are in
    `presto_comparison.py`'s docstring. What belongs here: every JS number this
    project has put beside presto's Table 1 so far has been on a different
    cohort. This one is not. The cohort now matches and the protocol still does
    not, since presto relaxes with the force field at every scan angle and this
    reads the QM geometry, so the row is comparable in cohort alone and the
    figure says which of the two caveats has gone.

    THE ARMS ARE ARGUMENTS. The fine-tuned arm of job 79462 runs an hour and a
    half behind the frozen one, so this takes `name=directory` pairs and reports
    on whatever has landed rather than refusing until both have.

    COST. Seconds, CPU, on files already pulled. No GPU.
    """
    input:
        script="presto_comparison.py",
        frozen_molecules="data/tn500_benchmark/frozen/per_molecule.csv",
        frozen_profiles="data/tn500_benchmark/frozen/profiles.csv",
        finetuned_molecules="data/tn500_benchmark/finetuned_reference/per_molecule.csv",
        finetuned_profiles="data/tn500_benchmark/finetuned_reference/profiles.csv",
    output:
        table="data/presto_comparison.csv",
        figure="results/presto_comparison.png",
    log:
        "logs/presto_comparison.log",
    shell:
        """
        {ESPALOMA} {input.script} \\
          --arms frozen=data/tn500_benchmark/frozen \\
                 finetuned=data/tn500_benchmark/finetuned_reference \\
          --table {output.table} \\
          --figure {output.figure} > {log} 2>&1
        """


rule tn500_arms:
    """Frozen against fine-tuned inside job 79462, paired on the same molecules.

    WHY THIS EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM `full_pool_arms`. That rule pairs the two
    arms of job 79460 and found fine-tuning a small harm: 1,849 wins of 3,889,
    sign test p = 0.0023. Job 79462 reran the same two arms under the same seed
    and hyperparameters to add TorsionNet500, and the sign REVERSED. Pairing
    inside each run is still the right comparison; what the two rules together
    show is that the between-run difference is larger than the between-arm one,
    which no single run can say and which neither rule alone would have caught.

    The fine-tuned arm of 79462 stopped at epoch 399 of a 400 cap with its best
    validation on the last epoch, so it was epoch-limited rather than converged;
    in 79460 the same configuration plateaued at 288. That is the mechanism to
    check before either sign is believed.

    COST. Seconds, CPU. No GPU.
    """
    input:
        script="compare_arms.py",
        frozen_molecules="data/tn500_benchmark/frozen/per_molecule.csv",
        finetuned_molecules="data/tn500_benchmark/finetuned_reference/per_molecule.csv",
        frozen_profiles="data/tn500_benchmark/frozen/profiles.csv",
        finetuned_profiles="data/tn500_benchmark/finetuned_reference/profiles.csv",
        frozen_loss="data/tn500_benchmark/frozen/loss.csv",
        finetuned_loss="data/tn500_benchmark/finetuned_reference/loss.csv",
    output:
        molecules="data/tn500_benchmark/both_per_molecule.csv",
        profiles="data/tn500_benchmark/both_profiles.csv",
        loss="data/tn500_benchmark/both_loss.csv",
        curves="results/tn500_curves.png",
        before_after="results/tn500_before_after.png",
        profile_figure="results/tn500_profiles.png",
        summary="data/tn500_arms.csv",
    log:
        "logs/tn500_arms.log",
    shell:
        """
        for pair in "{input.frozen_molecules} {input.finetuned_molecules} {output.molecules}" \
                    "{input.frozen_profiles} {input.finetuned_profiles} {output.profiles}" \
                    "{input.frozen_loss} {input.finetuned_loss} {output.loss}"; do
          set -- $pair
          head -1 "$1" > "$3"
          tail -n +2 "$1" >> "$3"
          tail -n +2 "$2" >> "$3"
        done
        {ESPALOMA} {input.script} \\
          --history {output.loss} \\
          --per-molecule {output.molecules} \\
          --profiles {output.profiles} \\
          --curves {output.curves} \\
          --before-after {output.before_after} \\
          --profile-figure {output.profile_figure} \\
          --summary {output.summary} > {log} 2>&1
        """
